course: history
lesson: 22
title: The Islamic golden age and the wider medieval world
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: While western Europe was building castles, some of the world's greatest centres of learning were in the Islamic world, from Spain to Central Asia.
explain: The prophet Muhammad lived in Arabia about 1,400 years ago, and within a hundred years Muslim rulers governed lands from Spain to India, where Arabic became the shared language of learning and trade.
explain: In Baghdad's House of Wisdom scholars of many faiths translated Greek, Persian and Indian books into Arabic, much as a group of friends today might translate a game into every language so everyone can play it.
explain: Many old Greek books survived only because of those Arabic copies, which were later turned into Latin and reached Europe's universities.
explain: Further south, West Africa had rich kingdoms too: Mansa Musa of Mali gave away so much gold on his journey to Mecca that the price of gold fell in Cairo.
fact: The prophet Muhammad lived in Arabia about 1,400 years ago, and Islam spread quickly until Muslim rulers governed lands from Spain to India.
fact: Arabic became the shared language of learning and trade across this huge area.
fact: Baghdad was founded in 762 and became one of the biggest cities in the world, a centre of trade, science and books.
fact: In Baghdad's House of Wisdom scholars translated Greek, Persian and Indian books into Arabic, and many Greek works survived this way before being put into Latin.
fact: The scholar al-Khwarizmi wrote a famous book on solving equations; the word algebra comes from its title and the word algorithm comes from his name; an algorithm is a list of steps for solving a problem, and every computer program runs on algorithms.
fact: The numbers 0 to 9 that we call Arabic numerals were first developed in India and reached Europe through Arabic books.
fact: The doctor Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, wrote a great medical encyclopedia that European universities used for hundreds of years.
fact: Ibn al-Haytham showed that we see because light enters our eyes, not because the eyes send out rays, and he tested his ideas by experiment.
fact: Muslim astronomers improved the astrolabe, an instrument for telling the time and finding direction by the stars, and star names such as Aldebaran and Betelgeuse come from Arabic.
fact: In Muslim Spain, called al-Andalus, cities such as Córdoba had great libraries, and Granada has the palace of the Alhambra; Muslims, Christians and Jews often lived side by side there.
fact: Muslims learned papermaking from China and built paper mills in Baghdad and later in Spain, so books became cheaper.
fact: Ibn Battuta from Morocco travelled for nearly 30 years, about 700 years ago, through Africa, the Middle East, India and China, and wrote a book about it.
fact: Mansa Musa, ruler of the Mali Empire in West Africa, made his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 and gave away so much gold that its price fell in Cairo.
fact: Timbuktu in Mali had schools and libraries, and many of its old handwritten books still survive.
exercise: Where did the prophet Muhammad live? | In Arabia | In Spain, near Granada | In China, by the sea | In England
exercise: Which language was shared across the Islamic world for learning and trade? | Arabic | Latin, like in Rome | Chinese | English
exercise: Which city, founded in 762, became a great centre of learning? | Baghdad | Rome | Constantinople | Jerusalem
exercise: What did scholars do in the House of Wisdom? | Translated books from many languages into Arabic | Trained knights to fight in the Crusades | Built ships for explorers | Printed newspapers for the people of the city
exercise: How did many old Greek books survive the Middle Ages? | They were translated into Arabic and later into Latin | They were buried in Greek caves | Knights carried them home inside their armour | They were carved on castle walls in Spain and France
exercise: Which two words come from al-Khwarizmi? | Algebra and algorithm | Alphabet and alarm | Atlas and astronaut | Album, almond and apricot
exercise: Where were the numbers 0 to 9 first developed? | In India | In Rome, by Romans | In England | In Mali, by traders
exercise: Who was Ibn Sina? | A great doctor who wrote a medical encyclopedia | A Viking king who sailed to Baghdad | A Mongol general | A Roman emperor
exercise: How do we see things, as Ibn al-Haytham showed? | Light enters our eyes | Our eyes send out rays | Our brain makes pictures without light | Sound waves hit our eyes
exercise: What was an astrolabe used for? | Telling the time and finding direction by the stars | Weighing gold coins | Measuring the rain and the floods of the river | Cooking food on ships
exercise: What is the Alhambra? | A palace in Granada in Spain | A great mosque built in Baghdad | A ship of Ibn Battuta | A book on algebra
exercise: Where did Muslims learn to make paper? | From China | From Rome | From the Vikings | From England
exercise: Where was Ibn Battuta from? | Morocco | Norway | China | Venice in Italy
exercise: Why did gold become cheaper in Cairo? | Mansa Musa gave away so much of it | A gold mine opened right under the city | The Vikings stole all the gold | People stopped liking gold
exercise: Which city in Mali had famous libraries? | Timbuktu | Venice | Kyiv | Constantinople
test: dude says Arabic numerals were invented in Arabia obviously its in the name. right? | No, they were first developed in India | Yes, they come only from Arabia and nowhere else | No, they come from Rome | Yes, Mansa Musa invented them
test: Your little sister thinks eyes shoot out beams like a superhero to see. Who proved this wrong long ago? | Ibn al-Haytham, who showed light enters the eyes | Mansa Musa, who studied gold and how it shines | Genghis Khan, who rode horses | Ibn Battuta, who travelled to China
test: Why does every computer program depend on a word from a scholar in Baghdad? | Programs run on algorithms, named after al-Khwarizmi | Computers were invented in Baghdad | Every program is written in Arabic | Al-Khwarizmi built the very first computer out of brass and wood
test: What would have happened to many Greek books if nobody had translated them into Arabic? | Many would probably have been lost for ever | They would have been written in English | Nothing, everyone in Europe read Greek | They would have turned into Latin by themselves
test: A cartoon shows medieval Africa as only jungle with no towns. What should you put right? | Africa had rich kingdoms like Mali and cities with libraries like Timbuktu | Africa had no people then | Only Europeans built cities | It is completely accurate
test: You look up at the star Betelgeuse. Where does its name come from? | Arabic, from medieval astronomers | Latin, from the Roman soldiers who watched the sky | English, from Shakespeare | Chinese, from Kublai Khan
test: Why would cheap paper help learning? | More books could be made, so more people could read them | Paper made people cleverer all by itself, without reading | Paper replaced teachers | Paper was used only for money
test: In al-Andalus, who often lived side by side? | Muslims, Christians and Jews | Only Muslims | Only Vikings and Mongols who came to trade | Nobody, it was empty
test: How did Ibn Battuta's book help historians today? | It describes many lands of the medieval world he saw | It was a book of sums and puzzles | It gave the rules of jousting for knights | It was a map of the Moon
test: why'd everyone go to baghdad back then, nothing to do there surely | It was a huge city of trade, science and books | It was a small fishing village on a muddy river bank | It was only a castle for knights | Nobody went there
exam: Identify the scholar from whose name the term algorithm is derived. | Al-Khwarizmi | Ibn Sina | Ibn Battuta | Mansa Musa
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contribution of the House of Wisdom in Baghdad? | The translation of Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic | The construction of castles throughout Europe | The invention of the printing press | The conquest of China
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the price of gold in Cairo subsequent to the pilgrimage of Mansa Musa. | It fell, owing to the quantity of gold he distributed | It rose sharply, owing to a shortage throughout the region | It remained wholly unaltered | Gold was thereafter prohibited
exam: Ascertain the land in which the numerals nought to nine, commonly termed Arabic, were originally developed. | India | Arabia | Greece | Spain and Portugal
